{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792434763,"nonce":"lj2ZHSVBPPYVxWpIzd1BBuCog54=","message":"Investigated the user's continuing ordinary-code-tab delay with GC debug logging unset. Source file loading and openFile itself were small (approximately 2-4 ms); most offscreen time was CodeEditor construction/reactive flush, layout, and the first GPU drawing work. A 2700-line Workbench.hx sample in a small fixture took about 692 ms over the open action and three frames (approximately 576 ms through the first draw submission). This does not reproduce the user's full roughly three-second real-window delay.\n\nImplemented RayDE Explorer folders with Ashui TreeItem.load instead of recursively constructing every descendant UI element. Folder rows and files load on first expansion, then retain their nodes while that item remains mounted. Refresh rebuilds from current filesystem contents while preserving expansion signals. This is particularly relevant to RayDE's build.hxml, which includes sibling Ashui classpaths; current src and those two Ashui classpaths contain 428 Haxe files.\n\nFocused source/tree probe options: RAYDE_PROBE_SOURCE copies one sample into the isolated fixture, and RAYDE_PROBE_FOLDERS adds ten files under each initially closed source folder. In the 100-folder / 1000-file fixture at 1280x800 scale 1, baseline nodes fell from 6998 to 998; Explorer descendants from 6733 to 733. Baseline UI setup trace fell from about 1505 ms to 447 ms. Opening the small Main.hx sample through first offscreen draw submission was 557.2 ms before versus 456.1 ms after. Over all three frames it was 696.1 ms before and 716.6 ms after; scheduled diagnostic process startup and subsequent drawing make that longer interval variable, so do not claim a general end-to-end percentage improvement. Artifacts: bin/code-open-tree-before/results.json and bin/code-open-tree-after/results.json. The lazy-node reduction is deterministic; timings are individual traces.\n\nCompiled the same snapshot program to native code without an Ash/Rust rebuild. With the same fixture and runtime library, the native trace was 156.4 ms through first draw submission and 349.0 ms over the three frames. Native CodeEditor flush was about 64 ms, layout 46 ms, first draw 46 ms. Offscreen draw submission is not a real-window presentation or GPU-completion latency measurement. Ash executable SHA-256 7bf195ecc8e61a3f55fe3440a1f5d0a095f5a81dd6ff177e5552b09c4cf4315f; both libhl.dylib and libash_std.dylib SHA-256 5cb2dc433f6c8691bdf9033e3c04ad0a338dd1fd63254f81f821e24cb023de5d. Artifacts: bin/code-open-tree-native/results.json and its run.log. No new Cargo target or sibling source edit.\n\nValidation: basic and Git snapshots passed in both schemes. Added explicit checks that refreshing a closed folder does not instantiate its file rows; expanding it loads and selects the active file; closing and reopening retains the same row identity. Basic filesystem actions and diagnostics still pass, and the snapshots were inspected. scripts/rayde.sh --build, scripts/rayde.sh --native --build (existing newer Ash), standard macOS packaging, Python compile, shell syntax and git diff --check passed. bin/rayde is rebuilt and bin/RayDE.app remains the standard bytecode bundle. The approved root launcher passed its smoke test, but it, the untracked root runtime copies and original gc.log subsequently disappeared during final checks without a delete command from this work. Those paths were not recreated; use bin/rayde for the current native preview.\n\nThe overall memory/latency issue stays open. Further cold CodeEditor construction and first-use glyph/path shader costs remain visible even in the small offscreen fixture. The existing ASHUI_FRAME_LOG=\u003cfile\u003e window trace can distinguish events, scheduler, reactive flush, layout, GPU submission and present timing when checking the real-window delay after restart.\n\nSeparate pre-change probe observation: an initial focused Workbench.hx three-reopen trial failed on the second open with Null access at Owner.run -\u003e For.hx:44 -\u003e Watch.runQueued -\u003e LayoutTree.flush, after the first successful open and close. Saved log: bin/code-open-workbench-before/run.log. The focused probe now intentionally measures one first open/close, and ordinary basic reopen coverage passes. Do not treat that single failure as a diagnosed cause of this delay or as fixed by lazy Explorer loading.","files":null}]}